{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>He offered his offering.</b> All this is superfluous, since it already said ״הִקְרִיב נְתַנְאֵל״. Rather, the intention is to magnify the additional dimensions that accrue through the remembrance of the offering of Naḥshon ben Amminadav. And you will find that regarding Naḥshon it mentioned the remembrance of the offering three times: <b>the one who offered</b> etc., <b>his offering</b> etc., and <b>and his offering</b>. One is the remembrance of his drawing near to be first; one is for the dimension of the concealed offering; and one is for the offering itself. Likewise in the case of the prince of Torah it mentioned three offerings: <b>נְתַנְאֵל offered</b> etc., <b>he offered his offering</b> — whereas in the case of the other princes it mentioned only the remembrance of the offering brought to the House of HaShem, for they were unable to do like the kings of Israel, who are the tribe of Judah and the tribe of Issachar." "And the reason for its saying ״הִקְרִב״ without a yod is to expound the Masoretic tradition that it is an expression of command and exhortation. The intention in this is according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Sifrei 52) that Reuben contested him; therefore the Master of authority came and said <b>הִקְרִב</b> — therefore it is written without a yod to allude to this."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:35.894064634Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}